    "fb55aed6-59e5-446f-a45e-89fb00ab4761": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "template--19962249675099__fb55aed6-59e5-446f-a45e-89fb00ab4761-collapsible_row-1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "I'm using a hormonal contraceptive",
            "row_content": "<p>While you can certainly take the hormone test, it's important to note that hormonal contraceptives, such as implants and pills, can affect hormone levels. <br/><br/>Since hormonal contraceptives regulate your hormone balance, the effects observed in the hormone test might not be as distinct as in individuals not using hormonal birth control. </p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "template--19962249675099__fb55aed6-59e5-446f-a45e-89fb00ab4761-collapsible_row-2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "I'm on a fertility treatment",
            "row_content": "<p>While you can certainly take the hormone test, it's important to note that  fertility treatments often involve precise hormone manipulation that can influence hormonal test results. </p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "template--19962249675099__fb55aed6-59e5-446f-a45e-89fb00ab4761-collapsible_row-3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "I have PCOS or Endometriosis",
            "row_content": "<p>At Monthlies, we recognize that individuals dealing with conditions such as PCOS and endometriosis seek in-depth insights into their hormonal health. </p><p>While the Monthlies Hormone Test can provide valuable information, it may not fully encompass the complexity of hormonal issues related to PCOS or endometriosis.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        }
      },
